[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 703,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 466,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105782,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 466,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A capillary malformation (CM) is a defect in vasculogenesis resulting in the dilatation of cutaneous capillaries and/or postcapillary venules, affecting the skin and mucosa with frequent extension to the hypodermis and occasionally to deeper structures.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 704,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 467,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105783,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 467,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 705,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 468,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105784,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 468,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>LA90.0Z Capillary malformation, unspecified</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 706,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 469,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105785,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 469,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> for naevus simplex: naevus flammeus neonatorum; stork bite; angel&rsquo;s kiss; salmon patch; butterfly patch; for capillary malformation: port-wine stain.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 707,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 470,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105786,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 470,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Naevus simplex</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 708,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 471,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105787,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 471,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>CMs are most commonly located in the head and neck region, but they also occur on the trunk and extremities.</p>\r\n<p>Naevus simplex (NS) occurs in the forehead, eyelids, glabella, upper lip, nape of the neck, occiput, upper back, and lumbosacral region.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105788,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>NS comes to clinical attention at birth as one or more pink to red macules that fade with time; if it persists, it is called a CM. Those in the central forehead, cervical, and lumbosacral regions may be associated with underlying abnormalities of the CNS or spine. The best-known CM is the facial port-wine stain, which manifests usually unilaterally in one or more trigeminal sensory dermatomes and is sometimes accompanied by overgrowth of adjacent mucosa, soft tissue, and bone. Upper facial involvement extending to ocular tissues, leptomeninges, and the cerebral cortex constitutes Sturge&ndash;Weber syndrome {26055853}.</p>\r\n<p>CM also commonly occurs in simple or combined vascular malformations (lymphatic, venous, lymphatic-venous, arteriovenous), in <em>PIK3CA</em>-related overgrowth spectrum conditions such as Klippel&ndash;Tr&eacute;naunay syndrome and CLOVES syndrome (congenital lipomatous overgrowth, vascular malformations, epidermal naevi, and scoliosis or skeletal/spinal anomalies), and in other clinically and genetically distinct vascular entities {26055853; 27456075; 25557259; 25864701; 31111168; 31693582}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105789,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>NS is present in the majority of newborns, with no sex predilection. CM is usually evident at birth, with no sex predilection.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105790,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A somatic <em>GNAQ</em> mutation is present in Sturge&ndash;Weber syndrome, and germline or somatic mutations, particularly in <em>PIK3CA</em>, are present in many conditions and syndromes with a CM component {23656586; 25557259; 25864701; 27456075}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105791,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>GNAQ</em> mutations have been shown to constitutively activate MAPK and/or YAP1 signalling, and <em>PIK3CA</em> mutations activate the AKT/mTOR signalling pathway {23656586; 29217067; 27259057}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105792,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em> (above).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105793,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Involved skin shows dispersed small channels with the appearance of dilated capillaries, postcapillary venules, and sometimes also small veins. At a young age, in the classic facial port-wine stain, only minimal vascular changes are present, but over time in some patients, channels become larger, with thicker (mostly fibrous) walls, and are accompanied by stromal fibrosis and appendageal hyperplasia. Cutaneous stains in lymphatic, venous, or lymphatic-venous malformations consist of dilated capillaries and/or postcapillary venules {21095470; 26055853}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105794,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105795,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>GNAQ</em> or <em>PIK3CA</em> mutations are the most commonly encountered findings.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105796,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> dilated capillaries and/or postcapillary venules in an appropriate clinical setting.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> detection of alterations in <em>GNAQ</em>, <em>PIK3CA</em>, and occasionally other genes is helpful in selected cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105797,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 105798,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 526,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Some CMs in the head and neck region progress with cutaneous and soft tissue thickening and nodularity.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-15T13:31:16"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]